{"user": "Hey, looking back - why did white keep passing after I played a3 and a1? I wasn't fully sure why that worked so well.", "user_ts": "2026-08-11T21:54:44.686964+00:00", "assistant": "White passed because **White had no legal move**, not because it chose to.\n\nIn Othello, a move is legal only if it **flanks at least one opponent disc**: White would need an empty square where a line of Black discs ends in a White disc. After your **a3** and then **a1**, the remaining empty squares didn\u2019t give White any such bracket, so White had **0 legal moves** and had to pass.\n\nThat worked well because:\n\n- **a1 is a corner**: once Black owns it, it can\u2019t be flipped.\n- The connected **a-file/top-edge Black discs became stable or hard to attack**.\n- You left White with no mobility, so you got extra consecutive moves to finish the board.\n\nSo the key idea was: you didn\u2019t just gain discs \u2014 you **removed White\u2019s ability to move**.", "assistant_started_ts": "2026-08-11T21:54:44.686964+00:00", "assistant_ts": "2026-08-11T21:54:49.686964+00:00", "annotation_user": {"final": ["Knowledge Deficit Question", "Metacomment"], "consensus": ["Knowledge Deficit Question", "Metacomment"], "votes": {"Knowledge Deficit Question": 3, "Metacomment": 2, "Think Aloud": 1}, "confidence": {"Knowledge Deficit Question": 1.0, "Metacomment": 0.6666666666666666, "Think Aloud": 0.3333333333333333}, "per_model": {"gpt": ["Knowledge Deficit Question", "Metacomment"], "llama": ["Think Aloud", "Knowledge Deficit Question"], "sonnet": ["Knowledge Deficit Question", "Metacomment"]}, "n_valid": 3, "min_votes": 2, "no_majority": false, "needs_review": false, "arbiter_used": false}, "annotation_assistant_fine": {"final": ["Board Report", "Local Justification", "General Principle", "Positive Feedback"], "consensus": ["Board Report", "Local Justification", "General Principle", "Positive Feedback"], "votes": {"Board Report": 3, "Local Justification": 3, "Worked Line": 1, "General Pri